<Layout title="Research · Weight Perturbation" description="A scientific overview of Akaash Bella’s CNN weight perturbation research, experimental design, and an accessible conceptual Gaussian perturbation visualization.">
  <PageTitle number="02" label="Research notes / In progress" title="Learning under perturbation." intro="How does training-time Gaussian weight noise affect clean classification performance and independently measured weight robustness?" />
  <div class="wrap research-byline"><span>Akaash Bella · Graduate Researcher</span><span>Oregon State University / 2026–present</span></div>
  <div class="wrap article-grid"><aside class="article-margin"><p class="eyebrow">Reading index</p><nav class="article-index" aria-label="Research article sections"><a href="#question">01 / The question</a><a href="#model">02 / The model</a><a href="#design">03 / The study</a><a href="#workflow">04 / The workflow</a><a href="#status">05 / Current status</a></nav><p class="caption">Scientific contract<br />main-v1 / schema 4</p></aside><article class="prose"><section id="question"><p class="eyebrow">01 / Motivation</p><h2>What does weight noise ask of a network?</h2><p>Clean classification and robustness to weight changes describe different properties of a trained network. This research investigates their relationship across convolutional architectures, using a controlled perturbation strength during training and a separate fixed-network test protocol.</p><p>The aim is a reproducible comparison, with validation and test roles kept distinct. The current work implements the experimental infrastructure; scientific computation has not been runtime-verified.</p></section><section id="model"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The perturbation model</p><h2>A temporary change.<br />A clean source of truth.</h2><div class="equation" aria-label="W prime equals W plus alpha times the population standard deviation of W times epsilon">W′ = W + <i>α</i> · σ(W) · ε</div><dl class="equation-key"><div><dt>W</dt><dd>The current clean/master weight tensor.</dd></div><div><dt>α</dt><dd>A fixed global perturbation strength for the training run.</dd></div><div><dt>σ(W)</dt><dd>Population standard deviation across the entire clean tensor, before noise.</dd></div><div><dt>ε</dt><dd>Independent standard-normal noise, sampled afresh for each eligible tensor and mini-batch during training.</dd></div></dl><p>Only convolutional and linear weight tensors are directly perturbed. Biases, normalization parameters, and buffers are excluded. Gradients at the temporary weights update the clean parameters; detached noise and scale do not accumulate in stored weights or optimizer state.</p><p>At α = 0, the implementation bypasses perturbation and noise sampling. Clean validation always uses the master weights.</p></section></article></div>
  <div class="wrap research-interaction"><Perturbation /></div>
  <div class="wrap article-grid"><aside class="article-margin"><p class="meta">Design note</p><p class="caption">240 is a planned training count.<br />6,720 is a planned evaluation-slot count.<br />Neither denotes completed runs.</p></aside><article class="prose"><section id="design"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Experimental design</p><h2>One grid. Two questions.</h2><p>The main design crosses two datasets, four architectures, ten training alphas, and three seeds. Each model is trained from scratch, without pretrained weights, for 200 epochs at batch size 128.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Planned main-v1 training grid</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Datasets</th><td>CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100; native 32 × 32 RGB images</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Architectures</th><td>ResNet50, MobileNetV3-Large, EfficientNet-B0, ConvNeXt-Tiny</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Training α</th><td>0, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.50, 0.75, 1.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Repetitions</th><td>Three training seeds per configuration; seed integers pending</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Training split</th><td>45,000 training / 5,000 validation images per dataset, shared across the grid</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Testing</th><td>All 10,000 official test images per realization</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Total</th><td>2 × 4 × 10 × 3 = 240 planned training runs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Architecture adaptations</h3><p>ResNet50 uses a 3 × 3, stride-1 stem and no initial max pool. MobileNetV3-Large and EfficientNet-B0 use stride 1 in the initial stem. ConvNeXt-Tiny uses a 3 × 3, stride-1 stem. Remaining structure and native regularization are preserved.</p><h3>Independent robustness evaluation</h3><p>The separate <code>weight_robustness_eval_v1</code> protocol binds each run’s best clean-validation checkpoint. It tests one clean realization and three independent realizations for each of nine positive evaluation alphas: 28 slots per checkpoint, 6,720 planned slots overall.</p><p>Unlike training, each perturbed test realization holds one complete noisy network fixed for the entire test pass. Whole-state checks protect the original parameters, buffers, bindings, and modes. Best training alpha is selected using mean clean validation accuracy across seeds; test results do not guide selection.</p></section></article></div>
