  <div class="wrap article-grid"><aside class="article-margin"><p class="meta">Reproducibility</p><p class="caption">Scientific identities, execution provenance, and scheduler state have separate responsibilities.</p></aside><article class="prose"><section><p class="eyebrow">04 / Engineering the experiment</p><h2>Reproducibility includes the interruptions.</h2><p>The source includes configuration validation and fingerprinting, isolated initialization, independent random streams, shared dataset preparation, and reproducible loaders. The temporary forward uses PyTorch’s functional parameter view, retaining ownership of clean master parameters.</p><p>Durable checkpoints, recovery, manifests, coverage inspection, Slurm task tracking, and numerical-failure records address long-running HPC work. A failed or incomplete result must remain visibly incomplete. Reporting requires complete prescribed seed and realization sets for official summaries.</p><p>Individual results remain primary. Supporting means and sample standard deviations keep training-seed variability separate from within-checkpoint evaluation variability. Missing or nonfinite values cannot silently become successful scores.</p></section><section id="status"><p class="eyebrow">05 / Current status</p><h2>Implemented in source.<br /><em>Still to be tested.</em></h2><div class="status-note"><strong>Research computation has not been runtime-verified.</strong><p>The README describes nine completed source steps and later focused operational queue-policy checks. These do not establish successful scientific execution or final results.</p></div><p>Before research execution, the documented open decisions include training seeds, dataset split and normalization policy, scheduler parameters and cadence, and precision/backend settings. HPC setup, runtime verification, training, and evaluation remain separate work.</p><p>No accuracy curves, robustness improvements, final paper, or scientific conclusions are claimed here. The interactive figure above is a mathematical illustration using example weights.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/weight-perturbation/">Read the implementation case study →</a></section></article></div>
